\right\| \\
&= \left\| \mathbf{a} \cdot (\mathbf{b} \times \mathbf{c}) + \mathbf{b} \cdot (\mathbf{b} \times \mathbf{c}), \end{align*}where we used the fact that if $\mathbf{p}$ and $\mathbf{q}$ are perpendicular vectors, then the projection of $\mathbf{p}$ onto $\mathbf{q}$ is 0; and the cross product of a vector with itself is 0.

Since $\mathbf{a}$ and $\mathbf{b} \times \mathbf{c}$ are perpendicular, the projection of $\mathbf{b}$ onto $\mathbf{b} \times \mathbf{c}$ is 0, so $\mathbf{b}$ and $\mathbf{b} \times \mathbf{c}$ are also perpendicular. Therefore, $\mathbf{b} \cdot (\mathbf{b} \times \mathbf{c}) = 0$.

Therefore, \[\left\| \mathbf{a} \cdot (\mathbf{b} \times \mathbf{c}) + \mathbf{b} \cdot (\mathbf{b} \times \mathbf{c})  \right\| = \left\| \mathbf{a} \cdot (\mathbf{b} \times \mathbf{c}) + 0 \right\| = \boxed{4}.\] </think> <answer> 4 </answer>

